Cap the buffer memDecompress(type = "gzip") allocates up front from the
gzip trailer when R is built with libdeflate, so the decompress target
stops dying on a forged ISIZE.

The libdeflate branch of do_memDecompress() (src/main/connections.c)
reads the uncompressed size recorded in the gzip trailer and R_alloc()s
it before decoding a byte.  The trailer is part of the input, so a 1 KB
stream can claim 4 GB; libdeflate would reject the stream as bad data,
but the allocation happens first:

    ==248== ERROR: libFuzzer: out-of-memory (malloc(4026531936))
        #8 Rf_allocVector3 /src/r-source/src/main/memory.c:2895:7
        #10 R_alloc /src/r-source/src/main/memory.c:2333:6
        #11 do_memDecompress /src/r-source/src/main/connections.c:7299:19

DEFLATE expands by at most 1032:1, so cap the initial buffer at that
multiple of the input; a truthful ISIZE is always within the cap and
output of 4 GB or more (where ISIZE has wrapped) is still reached by
the existing doubling on LIBDEFLATE_INSUFFICIENT_SPACE.  Also require
the 18 bytes of a minimal gzip member before reading the magic and the
trailer, so a shorter input is handed to libdeflate instead of read past
its end.

Found by OSS-Fuzz (testcase 5726025607348224).  The same input hit the
zlib path's infinite loop before the fuzz image gained libdeflate; that
fix is patch 0022 (r-svn PR #357, Bugzilla 19200).

Upstream status: r-devel/r-svn PR #358 (no Bugzilla report yet); its
regression test in tests/reg-tests-1e.R is left out here so this carry
patch does not go stale with that file.
Delete this patch once the fix lands in R trunk (ossfuzz.sh will report
it as already-applied in the meantime, which is harmless).

diff --git a/src/main/connections.c b/src/main/connections.c
index a4905eeecb..e4a6f2fd88 100644
--- a/src/main/connections.c
+++ b/src/main/connections.c
@@ -7291,16 +7291,26 @@ do_memDecompress(SEXP call, SEXP op, SEXP args, SEXP env)
         enum libdeflate_result res;
 	Bytef *buf, *p = (Bytef *)RAW(from);
 
-	if (p[0] == 0x1f && p[1] == 0x8b) { // in-memory gzip file
-	    while(1) {
-		outlen = get_unaligned_le32(&p[inlen - 4]);
-		if (outlen == 0) outlen = 1;
+	/* a gzip member is at least a 10-byte header plus the 8-byte
+	   trailer; anything shorter is left to libdeflate to reject */
+	if (inlen >= 18 && p[0] == 0x1f && p[1] == 0x8b) { // in-memory gzip file
+	    /* The trailer's ISIZE is the uncompressed length mod 2^32, but
+	       it is part of the input and cannot be trusted: a 1 KB
+	       stream can claim 4 GB and that is allocated before a byte
+	       is decoded.  DEFLATE expands by at most 1032:1, so a size
+	       beyond that bound is a lie (or a stream of 4 GB or more,
+	       which the doubling below still reaches). */
+	    size_t maxlen = 1032 * inlen + 64;
+	    outlen = get_unaligned_le32(&p[inlen - 4]);
+	    if (outlen == 0) outlen = 1;
+	    else if (outlen > maxlen) outlen = maxlen;
 
+	    while(1) {
 		buf = (Bytef *) R_alloc(outlen, sizeof(Bytef));
 		res = libdeflate_gzip_decompress(d, RAW(from), inlen,
 						 buf, outlen, &actual_out);
 		if(res == LIBDEFLATE_INSUFFICIENT_SPACE) {
-		    // should not happen but recorded length might be wrong.
+		    // recorded length wrong, capped above, or output >= 4 GB
 		    if(outlen < ULONG_MAX/2) {
 			outlen *= 2; continue;
 		    } else break;
